{
  "$comment": "BLESSED RECORD — §8 rule 5 of engineering/decisions/2026-07-22-structure-derived-split-patterns.md. The `components` map is RECOMPUTED from the component manifests by tools/bless-split-oracle.js and gated by checkSplitOracle in tools/check-ownership.js (build:check). A diff there is a SPLIT-BEHAVIOR CHANGE to review, not noise: run `npm run oracle:bless` only when you intend it, and justify each moved entry in the PR. The `verified` map is AUTHORED, never recomputed — it is rule 11's precondition (the record documents a verified default, it never mints one), so an ENROLLED component must name the committed deck that exercises its split and who signed the derived facts off. Blessing REFUSES to write an entry for a newly-enrolled component that has neither. The components enrolled before that precondition landed are frozen in GRANDFATHERED (tools/bless-split-oracle.js), a shrink-only backlog, not an exemption.",
  "verified": {
    "content": {
      "deck": "examples/split-structure.md",
      "by": "#2004 (rendered at size: portrait, indaco, and read page by page)",
      "note": "Slide 2 -> cover p2, one bullet per page p3-p6, closing p7 carrying the key insight. Axis `item` derived from the rendered <ul>; a paragraph-only content slide resolves no collection and is left whole (slide 1 of the same deck)."
    },
    "list-criteria": {
      "deck": "examples/split-structure.md",
      "by": "#2004 (rendered at size: portrait, indaco, and read page by page)",
      "note": "Cover p8, one criterion per page, closing page carrying the italic annotation. The numbered row fills the measure -- this component is what caught the lone-member width fix (`width: 100%` sheared its text; `width: auto` is correct). soft/hard match what its own stress doc holds."
    },
    "journey": {
      "deck": "examples/portrait-journey.md",
      "by": "#2042 (rendered at size: story, indaco, and read page by page)",
      "note": "PORTRAIT ONLY, which is the whole enrollment. All three journey slides split to one `li.journey-vstage` per page (pages 6-8, 9-11, 12-14 of 15): stage band, that stage's rows with their actor dots and per-task mood faces, and BOTH legends repeated -- a page of mood faces without the mood key is unreadable. Tasks conserved 3+2+2, 2+3+1, 2+2+1 against the unsplit render; `data-section` stays 0/1/2 across each run, so each stage still resolves its own categorical token -- the attribute-driven property `timeline-list` lacks, which is why that one is placed atomic instead. BUT WHAT THAT BUYS ON THE PAGE IS SMALL, and the first wording of this note overclaimed it. Measured from computed style on the real render: all three bands paint the SAME background (a constant `--journey-stage-bg`, 81px tall) and the categorical token reaches only a 3px `border-top`. Unsplit, the three hairlines sit adjacent and read as a sequence; one stage per page leaves a single hairline with nothing to compare it against, which two independent visual reviewers each sampled and reported as 'the three bands are the same colour'. The stage NAME is set large and bold in the band, so no information is lost -- the colour is redundant encoding, and it is the redundancy that the split weakens. Not changed here: filling the band with the categorical colour would depart from the accent-rule-on-neutral idiom `roadmap` uses for the same job, which is a design decision about the engine's visual language rather than a defect in this split. The forward pointer names the next STAGE -- 'next: Trial', 'next: Activate' -- not an actor, verified against a 4-actor deck. LANDSCAPE is untouched and must stay so: `examples/data-viz-gallery.md` renders byte-identical through the split (sha 4c1f75b0-era A/B), because the grid form carries an absolute `--col` per task into `repeat(var(--task-count), 1fr)` and a slice would draw tasks into columns that are no longer there. `journeyStageSplit` returns null without an `ol.journey-vstack`, which is what enforces that. Reshape is `paginator`: the split paginates a vertical stack and cannot narrow anything, so it must not be marked width-reducing."
    }
  },
